---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can you get burial insurance with COPD?',
    a: '<p>Yes. Depending on your situation, that may be a plan with full coverage from day one, a graded plan, or a guaranteed issue plan, which asks no health questions. The last two have a waiting period.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does using oxygen disqualify me?',
    a: '<p>Not from every plan. Home oxygen usually rules out full coverage from day one, but guaranteed issue plans don’t ask about it. They have a waiting period and cost more.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a CPAP machine count as oxygen?',
    a: '<p>Usually not. Most insurers treat a CPAP for sleep apnea differently from home oxygen for lung disease. Tell us what you use and we’ll check how each insurer counts it.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a breathing test?',
    a: '<p>No. There are no exams or breathing tests for the final expense plans we offer. Approval depends on your answers to a few health questions and the insurer’s records check.</p>',
  },
];
---
<Guide
  title="Burial Insurance With COPD: What to Expect"
  h1="Burial insurance with COPD"
  description="Final expense insurance with COPD or emphysema: how insurers look at inhalers, oxygen and smoking, which plan is typical, and what to have ready."
  path="/burial-insurance-with-copd/"
  crumb="Burial insurance with COPD"
  eyebrow="Health conditions"
  lede="COPD narrows the choices, but it rarely closes them. Whether you use oxygen at home is the biggest dividing line."
  published="2026-11-07"
  reviewed="2026-11-07"
  summary={[
    "Many insurers ask whether you've been diagnosed with or treated for COPD or emphysema, often within the last two years.",
    'COPD without oxygen may still qualify for full coverage from day one with some insurers.',
    'Home oxygen (not a CPAP for sleep apnea) usually means a guaranteed issue plan with a waiting period.',
    'No medical exam. Approval depends on your answers to a few health questions.',
  ]}
>
  <h2>How insurers look at COPD</h2>
  <p>
    COPD, emphysema and chronic bronchitis are among the most common reasons for a plan with a waiting period, but the details matter.
    Insurers usually ask:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether you've been diagnosed with or treated for COPD, emphysema or chronic lung disease, and how recently.</li>
    <li>Whether you use oxygen at home.</li>
    <li>Whether you've been in the hospital for breathing problems recently.</li>
    <li>Whether you've used tobacco or nicotine in the last 12 months. That decides which price you pay.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Inhalers and other breathing medicines show up in the prescription check, so the insurer will know about them either way.</p>

  <h2>What's typical, by situation</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Typical outcomes with COPD. Each insurer sets its own rules, so this is a starting point, not a promise.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Your situation</th><th scope="col">What's typical</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Inhalers only, no oxygen, no recent hospital stays</th><td data-label="What's typical">Some insurers offer full coverage from day one; others offer a graded plan.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A hospital stay for breathing problems in the last year or two</th><td data-label="What's typical">Often a graded plan with a waiting period.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Home oxygen</th><td data-label="What's typical">Usually a guaranteed issue plan, with a waiting period.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Still using tobacco or nicotine</th><td data-label="What's typical">Tobacco prices apply, and some insurers are stricter with COPD if you still smoke.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What helps your application</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A list of your breathing medicines and inhalers,</strong> with doses.</li>
    <li><strong>Whether you use oxygen,</strong> and since when.</li>
    <li><strong>Dates of any hospital stays</strong> for breathing problems.</li>
    <li><strong>When you last used tobacco or nicotine,</strong> including vaping and nicotine patches or gum. Many insurers count those too.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Honest answers.</strong> During the first two years, an insurer can review the application if there's a claim, and a wrong
      answer can lead to a denied claim.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Quitting tobacco and your price</h2>
  <p>
    Most insurers charge non-tobacco prices once you've gone 12 months without tobacco or nicotine. If you're close to that mark, ask us
    whether it makes sense to wait. Waiting has its own risk if your health changes, so we'll weigh it with you.
  </p>

  <h2>If a plan with a waiting period is the best fit</h2>
  <p>
    On graded and guaranteed issue plans, if death from natural causes happens in the first two or three years, the insurer usually refunds
    the premiums paid, plus interest, instead of paying the full amount. Accidental death is usually covered in full from the start. After
    the waiting period, the full amount is paid. <a href="/guaranteed-issue-vs-simplified-issue/">How the plan types work</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Never cancel a policy you already have until the new one is approved and in force.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>Not sure how much coverage to get?</h3>
    <p>Add up the funeral and final bills in a minute with our <a href="/how-much-burial-insurance-do-i-need/">coverage calculator</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Questions about COPD and burial insurance</h2>
  <Faq items={faqs} />
</Guide>
